WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is
a Grammy Award-winning rapper,

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.404
activist, and half of the
hip-hop duo Run the Jewels.

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:07.706
Please welcome back to the show
Killer Mike.

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:11.244
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:14.113
-Seth, how you doing, brother?
-I'm doing well.

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:15.682
Thanks for being here so late.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:19.953
You are at least comfortably
at home, yes?

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.721
-No, I'm actually in the studio.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:24.423
I owed some guest verses out,
so I'm working the late shift.

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:27.360
I'm working third shift
at UPS like when I was 19.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.896
[ Both laugh ]
I'm in the studio all night.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:32.531
-Hey, while we're there,
and I know it will pale

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:34.066
in the significance
of everything

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:36.603
we're gonna talk about tonight,
but I do want to say

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:38.738
Run the Jewels put out
a great album last year,

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.173
and you should have been
nominated for a Grammy,

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:44.944
and you were robbed.
And I'm gonna leave it at that.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.046
-I'm gonna strongly co-sign.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.448
And thank you
for being a believer in us.

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.951
And with that said,
the people who were nominated

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.520
for Best Rap Album of the Year
all were deserving,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.189
and Run the Jewels
definitely should've been

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.691
Album of the Year, so I agree.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.192
Maybe next year.
We'll try again.

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:01.361
-You know what?

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:04.063
I would expect nothing less
from you two.

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:08.033
So, you know, last night
was obviously a night

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:11.171
of great optimism,
a night to celebrate,

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:14.173
and I want to talk about what
was accomplished in Georgia,

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:17.943
but I also want to ask,
on a day when it seemed like

00:01:17.943 --> 00:01:19.978
that would be
what the news cycle was,

00:01:19.978 --> 00:01:25.018
obviously, it was ripped away,
based on the incident,

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.886
the activities
that happened in D.C.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:30.523
What was your reaction
to what you saw in D.C.?

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.793
-Well, my reaction was,
I woke up knowing that Georgia

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:37.229
had done a heck of a job,

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:40.633
that everyone from the hip-hop
community in Atlanta

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.436
and throughout the state
had worked their butt off.

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.872
They had worked their butt off,
some, the progressives,

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.107
from 16 and forward,
they worked their butt off,

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:49.475
you know, in an alliance
with Keisha,

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.910
worked their butt off,
in an alliance with Stacey.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.680
What I really respected
about the integrity, though,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.882
of my state, beyond people
you'd think I would

00:01:56.882 --> 00:02:00.786
typically agree with,
I was very pleased to see

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:03.022
the integrity
of our Governor Kemp

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:06.926
and of Brad Raffensperger,
our Secretary of State.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.761
Because they called it
for Biden.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.863
They called it honest.
They called it fair.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:13.599
And I respected the fact that
my state did the right thing.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.067
The state went blue.

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:17.270
Republicans in the state didn't
try to get in the way of it.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.339
It happened.
So I woke up on a high.

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:20.873
I woke up knowing
that Warnock had won,

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:23.576
pretty sure that Ossoff was
gonna be called the winner.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.945
I woke up understanding that my
community, hip-hop community,

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.714
had helped put them there
in big part.

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:31.283
And I got a call from my
13-year-old a few hours ago

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.053
that, "Dad, this stuff was
happening at the Capitol."

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:37.056
And it led me into
a longer conversation with her

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:39.959
about how I admired the fact
that she participated

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.995
in the political process
by paying attention.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.596
I admired the fact
her 18-year-old brother

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.231
had voted for the first time.

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.299
And I really wanted her
to understand that the people

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:50.002
that were at the Capitol,
although I encourage protests

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:53.606
for any American -- right? --
I don't encourage what we saw.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.642
And, essentially,
it is violence, it is evil,

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.377
it is "I didn't get my wayism."

00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:02.181
It is the refusal to accept
that this republic

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:04.984
is ran by the power of the vote,
and people have voted

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.186
an older president out
and the president-elect in,

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:08.454
and we need to accept that.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.823
And I thought
that it was a great time

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.258
to teach her about civics
and to teach her that

00:03:13.258 --> 00:03:16.061
what we have done in this state
is more important

00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:17.430
than this ugly moment.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.000
And I hope that all Americans
plot, plan, strategize,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.503
organize, and mobilize
for a union post this.

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:26.371
We've done the Civil War once.

00:03:26.371 --> 00:03:28.441
It didn't work out well
for the region I grew up in

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:30.276
in the South,
and it didn't work out well

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.844
for this nation as a whole.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:34.980
We repaired past that,
although we didn't do

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.982
the perfect repair we deserved.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:39.084
We are on our way
to a more perfect union.

00:03:39.084 --> 00:03:41.554
But it will only happen
if we use times like these

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.789
as a way to cooperate
and collaborate

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:45.491
for the betterment
of this republic

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:49.161
instead of displaying the type
of behavior that we saw today.

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:51.964
-Many people were drawing
the distinction today

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:55.334
on social media
between the police presence

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.536
that was at the Capitol today,

00:03:57.536 --> 00:04:02.041
knowing full well that there was
going to be this rally,

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.744
with the police presence
that was in place

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:10.316
during Black Lives Matter's
protests in D.C. this summer,

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:13.219
during, you know,
the protests this summer.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:16.021
What were your thoughts
when you saw what looked to be

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:18.524
a pretty clear difference?

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:23.129
-We know that there's --
that justice is lopsided.

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.363
We know that.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:27.567
There has been this example
and dozens more,

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.168
and I would argue hundreds more.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:34.640
The question for me becomes,
at what point do we have to keep

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.044
using these stark examples
versus doing something about it?

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:39.912
At what point do we encourage

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:43.216
the demilitarization of police,
period?

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:47.587
At what point do we live out
what the people thought today,

00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:49.155
they thought
that they were doing

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:51.891
what was a free people
doing right by a republic?

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:53.959
At what point do we want
that for everyone?

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:55.461
Noam Chomsky said,
"If you don't believe

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:56.729
in free speech for those
you disagree with,

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.263
you don't believe in it at all."

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:01.200
And I am, as I grow older,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.801
understanding that
clearer and clearer.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:05.405
It is not only my right
to like the stuff I like,

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:07.706
it's my right
and it's everyone's right

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:10.443
to have to accept the fact that
people have the right to say,

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:11.877
people have the right to do,

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:13.346
so long as they aren't
harming others.

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.081
And in this country,
if we're ever gonna be fair,

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:16.982
we have to call the B.S.
where it is.

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:18.517
Today, what happened was B.S.

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:20.586
versus what happened
over the summer.

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:23.055
And at some point as a Republic,
we're gonna have to cooperate

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:25.357
and collaborate with those
that we disagree with

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:28.193
for the betterment and a more
perfect union in this republic.

00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:31.196
And until we do that honestly,
until we want for others

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:33.966
what we want for ourselves,
we will repeat this cycle

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:37.302
over and over again
and will make us inept.

00:05:37.302 --> 00:05:41.206
-You mentioned how the hip-hop
community was a large part

00:05:41.206 --> 00:05:45.010
of the activism and the voter
registration in Georgia.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.380
And we were talking
to Mayor Bottoms this summer,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.518
and I'd just seen her
speaking with you, with T.I.,

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:55.488
you know, during some of
the protests in Atlanta.

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.457
And she mentioned that you all
went to the same high school.

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.860
What is it like
have this relationship

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.795
with the mayor of your city?

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.230
-Frederick Douglass High School.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.666
Let me tell you,
70% of the schools in Atlanta

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:09.268
are named for Black educators
or emancipators.

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:11.036
We went to Frederick Douglass
High School.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:12.204
Frederick Douglass Astros.

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:14.240
I want to shout out
Dr. Samuel Hill,

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.008
who's in his 80s
and still around,

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.844
who was my principal,
and Dr. Lester Butts,

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.947
who was the principal before
him, was my mom's principal.

00:06:20.947 --> 00:06:23.949
There's a tradition of creating
excellence in this high school.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:26.518
Keisha Lance Bottoms, T.I.,
Killer Mike,

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:28.720
Chief Judge Asha Jackson
in DeKalb County,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.424
Michael Julian Bond,
city councilperson,

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:34.159
Cameron Dollar,
former national champion, UCLA,

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:36.762
and Coach out in Seattle,
Stanley Pritchett,

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:38.464
Robert Hicks, my best friend
since kindergarten

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:41.500
whose mom, Miss Hicks,
was the school secretary.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.837
It really was a small-town vibe
in the midst of a big city.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.739
It produced excellence.
We were taught

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.207
from the day we got there,
without struggle

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:51.110
there is no process --
there is no progress.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.379
And Frederick Douglass also
said, "It's easier to build

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:55.681
strong children
than repair broken men."

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:58.584
He was an amazing author
and orator.

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:00.920
We were raised in his lineage
and image,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.355
and I'm proud to be
a Frederick Douglass Astro.

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:08.360
And I also want to say,
beyond just our mayor,

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.430
who's an amazing Black woman,
and beyond Stacey Abrams,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.198
who went to Spelman,
my sister college --

00:07:13.198 --> 00:07:15.334
I went to Morehouse --
who's an amazing black woman,

00:07:15.334 --> 00:07:16.936
I want to make sure Nina Turner,
who's gonna be

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:19.705
running for Congress out of Ohio
gets her props.

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.340
She's a person that pushed me

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.276
to push Bernie Sanders
to support Stacey Abrams.

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:26.245
Stacey Abrams was pushing,
although she did not

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:27.880
get a chance to be
the governor of Georgia,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.183
she's a big part of the reason
that this state did turn blue.

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:35.721
Her, Keisha, Warnock, Ossoff,
myself, the hip-hop community.

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:38.390
But I don't want to leave out
someone who's not in Georgia

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:41.160
but definitely has the cut
to be, and that's Mina Turner.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.997
So I want to encourage people
to get behind her in Ohio,

00:07:44.997 --> 00:07:47.333
and let's make sure that
she gets to Congress, as well.

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:48.767
-All right.
We'll be right back

00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.570
with more Killer Mike
after this.

